{
  "result": "At the edge of a small harbor, there is a bench painted blue so many times that the wood shows through in pale streaks. Each morning, an old man arrives with a thermos and a newspaper he rarely reads. He sits facing the water, watching fishing boats leave one by one, their engines coughing briefly before settling into a patient hum.\n\nNo one knows exactly why he comes. The bakery owner thinks he once worked on the sea. The schoolchildren believe he is a retired spy. A woman who walks her dog says he is simply lonely. The man offers no correction. He nods to everyone and pours coffee into a small lid.\n\nWhen the tide is low, he watches crabs make quick decisions among the rocks. When it rains, he opens a yellow umbrella and remains, as though the weather has made an appointment with him.\n\nOne afternoon, a girl drops her paper boat into the harbor. It spins uncertainly, catches a current, and drifts beyond the pilings. The old man smiles.\n\n\u201cIt knows where it is going,\u201d he says.\n\nThe girl frowns. \u201cBut I didn\u2019t tell it.\u201d\n\n\u201cThat is often the best way,\u201d he replies.\n\nYears later, perhaps she will remember only the blue bench, the coffee smell, and a sentence she did not understand. Yet some words wait quietly inside us, like boats at slack tide, until the water decides to carry them somewhere new. And perhaps then they become a direction for us all.",
